WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.150
[Scale Swap Challenge:]

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:04.765
[We asked three imaging specialists to&nbsp;
swap scales and share their results.]

00:00:04.765 --> 00:00:06.872
[#1: Astro to Photo]

00:00:07.881 --> 00:00:11.280
[Clara Sousa-Silva, Quantum Astrochemist]

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.080
Clara Sousa-Silva: So what you see here is the high&nbsp;
resolution CCD solar spectrum. So that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.560
basically the rainbow of the sun in its full&nbsp;
detail, and this was obtained professionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.080
not by me, by the current team at Harvard.&nbsp;
And I think about this image all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.600
I have this framed in my house you know, there's&nbsp;
just a complexity to all these lines and each line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.280
corresponds to an atom or a&nbsp;
molecule at the edge of the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:44.640
and as a spectroscopist in space we're just&nbsp;
always counting photons. And so I thought, okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.160
for my new scale I'm going to look at something&nbsp;
that has an abundance of photons. So I was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.560
trying to look at the sun too but with my own&nbsp;
handheld spectrometer that I keep in my purse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:01.440
just in case an interesting light source comes&nbsp;
my way. And I felt similarly to Jess that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.520
was an elusiveness to these images. I was trying&nbsp;
to capture both the sky and the spectrum of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.520
from the sun and you can see that all of those&nbsp;
millions, actually billions, of absorption lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.960
from the previous picture completely disappear&nbsp;
and it just looks like a continuous rainbow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:26.000
And I can imagine for the first people who saw the&nbsp;
the lines in the spectrum of the sun, apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.040
so the story goes, they thought it was dead&nbsp;
they thought it was a problem with their prism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.760
and I can totally imagine that because from this&nbsp;
perspective the sun just seems like a continuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:42.240
source of light, no interruptions and then what&nbsp;
I found was it was almost impossible to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.200
the spectrum and the sky in focus which of course&nbsp;
makes sense to anyone here who does photography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.680
but that hadn't even crossed my mind. In my&nbsp;
mind this photograph would have been kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:58.160
a beautiful, just adjust a position, and instead I&nbsp;
could only focus on the spectrum. So then I tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:07.200
something close to home and I saw that the light&nbsp;
in my hallway was old, and old lights tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:13.440
not be, you know, LED continued spectrum and I&nbsp;
was not disappointed so it's just a beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:20.080
emission spectrum and this was such a pleasure&nbsp;
to actually try and capture, but the elusiveness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.240
was so so true at this point I was trying to get&nbsp;
the camera aligned with the spectroscope and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.520
light hitting just right and I was trying to get&nbsp;
the echo of the spectra, the edge's spectrometer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.560
and even though I had an abundance of&nbsp;
photons I didn't have any stability and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.960
I really struggled with this and I have a whole&nbsp;
new appreciation for the instruments I use and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:03:03.840
architecture and engineering that goes into them&nbsp;
because spectra is hard at every scale it seems.

